; Fisher's waterbomb base through both layers in one statement, the variant
; his syntax cannot tell apart from flatten-waterbomb-top-layer.bel
; [fisher1994, §5.3]. The creases are marked on both layers, and the fan
; keeps the sector along the raw edges: the spine's pieces lie outside it and
; hinge the two layers together, so the tip is both layers (ADR 0037).
; `(staying .b .c)` names that sector by the two corners of the top layer,
; the anchor, that lie on its bounding rays (ADR 0048). The valley letters
; on the diagonals keep one of the five layer orders that fan leaves.

paper rectangle 2 1
fold (map .a onto .b) (moving .b) as --spine
.s0 = --spine * --ab
.s1 = free on --spine from .s0 at 1
mark (through .a .s1) as --d1
mark (through .s0 .d) as --d2
mark (map .a onto .s0) as --v
.m = (--d1 & .a) * --d2
.e = free on --ab from .a at 1/4
.f = free on --ab from .a at 3/4
flatten (--d1 & .a valley) (--d1 & .s1 valley) (--d2 & .s0 valley) (--d2 \ .s0 valley)
  (--v & --ab mountain) (--v \ --ab mountain) (staying .b .c)

; assert steps = 2
; assert faces = 12
; assert .a = (0, 0)
; assert .d = (0, 1)
; assert .b = (0, 0)
; assert .c = (0, 1)
; assert .m = (1/2, 1/2)
; assert .s0 = (0, 0)
; assert .s1 = (0, 1)
; assert .e = (0, 1/2)
; assert .f = (0, 1/2)
